  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2-x}2y\\,dy\\,dx.$",
  "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2-x}2y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2-x}2y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{y}^{2}]_{{x}^{2}}^{2-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((2-x)^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-4x+4)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-4x+4)\\,dx & =-{[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}+4x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}-2+4-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
